coverage.checked:
All against the frozen build at http://127.0.0.1:58667 (commit efe8710). Scripts, JSON outputs and screenshots are in /tmp/onebook-audit-0924/layout/ (scripts: *.mjs; data: out/*.json; images: shots/, out/*.png).

EXHAUSTIVE (every page a machine could reach):
(1) A static DOM pass over all 6,096 pages: /, /authors/, /shelf/, 1,837 author pages and 4,256 work pages (static-pass.mjs → out/static.json). It checked the h1 count, skipped heading levels, missing alt, unnamed controls, duplicate ids, foreign-script text with no lang, and RTL text with no dir. Result: every page has exactly one h1, there are 0 heading skips, 0 missing alt and 0 duplicate ids. The 1,837 'unnamed link' hits were false positives (links inside closed <details>, whose innerText is empty); I verified them and discarded them.
(2) A layout pass over all 6,096 pages at 320px and again at 360px (exhaustive.mjs → out/exh-320.json, out/exh-360-fonts.json). It measured document overflow, per-element overflow beyond the viewport, clipped text, and glyph-box intersections between different text nodes. Result: 0 horizontal overflow, 0 clipping. The overlaps are grouped by selector pair: the h1/orig/label box overlaps and the Korean jamo seal overlaps are line-box only, and I looked at the screenshots to confirm that.
(3) A font pass over all 6,096 pages: CDP CSS.getPlatformFontsForNode on h1, .orig, the 원제로 찾기 links, td.pub, relation names, index names, work titles, the dock and figcaptions. It found 1,633 nodes drawn in more than one font, grouped by cause.
(4) Edition tables: every one of the 758 pages with edition rows (1,796 rows), at 320, 360, 375, 390, 412, 414, 720, 768, 800, 834, 899, 900, 1024 and 1280. It measured ISBN or out-of-print text overlapped by links, and year, translator and publisher words split mid-word, not counting breaks after a hyphen (eds.mjs, eds2.mjs).

STRATIFIED SAMPLE: 58 page-states (sample.mjs → out/sample.json), each at 320/375/414/768/1280 in light and dark, for 580 loads:
- front, both empty and returning;
- the index;
- the shelf, empty and full (20 marks across 4 buckets, including the longest titles);
- 7 reviewed plates: lu-xun (vertical CJK), plato, taras-shevchenko, abai-qunanbaiuly, murasaki-shikibu, william-shakespeare, hermann-hesse;
- 11 draft plates: borges and kafka (most relations), dostoevsky, tagore (Bengali), mahfouz (Arabic), rumi (Persian), manto (Urdu), premchand (Devanagari), aristotle (Greek), murakami (8 works), akutagawa;
- 13 sketches: the longest original name, the longest Korean name, Hebrew, Greek, Cyrillic, Bengali, Ethiopic, Tamil, Thai, vertical Han, vertical Japanese, Arabic;
- both silhouettes;
- 18 work pages (6-edition Molloy, the longest Korean title, the longest original title, Japanese, Bengali, Hebrew, Arabic, Greek, Cyrillic, a silhouette's work), plus 2 in the marked state.
Measured: overflow, clipping, overlap, tap targets (14,585 target instances; WCAG 2.5.8 spacing and inline exceptions applied), contrast from computed rendered colours with opacity and alpha compositing (49,748 text elements per scheme), and placeholders.

OTHER CHECKS:
- Light vs dark: all 287 readable screenshot pairs are pixel-identical, because the page declares color-scheme: light. Forced dark was tested separately (forcedark*.mjs).
- Keyboard: 116 walks (58 pages × 375 and 1280, 2,258 Tab stops). Every stop showed a visible ring (the 2px seal outline, or the parent box-shadow on the door and search inputs). The number of stops equals the visible tabbables on every page, so the door input and button, mark buttons, ladder, nav and summaries are all reachable (the index walk was capped at 60 stops). A second walk of 22 pages at 375 (517 stops) waited for the scroll to settle before measuring whether focus is hidden.
- WCAG 1.4.12 text spacing: 58 pages at 320 and 375.
- Stamp sweep: the 43 longest-titled works, marked, at 9 desktop widths from 900 to 1920 (387 layouts).
- Reduced motion: CSS review, plus an observed smooth scroll during the keyboard walk.
- Screenshots I looked at: all measurement flags (ISBN, out-of-print, stamp, h1/orig, seals, fonts). Unflagged, per page type: front 375, shelf-full 375, hyegyeonggung-hong 320, naguib-mahfouz 375, natalio-hernandez 375, the forced-dark front, work and author pages, and the tablet and desktop edition tables.

findings.impact:
The only stated rescue works only when the magic link opens in the same browser profile that holds the marks. That fails when:
- the reader asked from an in-app browser (mail links never reopen inside KakaoTalk);
- they use Gmail or Naver Mail apps, which open links in their own in-app browser or a different default browser;
- they asked on one device and opened the mail on another.
In those cases B even reports '도감이 서버에도 있다' over an empty doc while the real marks stay unsynced in A. That breaks 지어내지 않는다 in the one place the site makes a promise about the reader's data.
